<!-- The FACTS clarity pass, chunk f0, 2026-10-22, the judge's final text: the blocks of proposals.apply for the entries applied (F-151, F-263, F-264), copied exactly and in the same order, for record.py apply by the coordinator. -->

- F-151 **In the interpreter's library, `Vector`, `Matrix` and their factories and operators bound their element type by `T extends { Number, MultiplicativeRing[\T\] }`.** `Number` declares no arithmetic, so the compiled checker accepts the bodies' arithmetic only through `MultiplicativeRing[\T\]`. `Number` stays in the bound because its `comprises` clause lists every type below it, so an element type excludes `String`; the ring alone does not give that. That exclusion makes the four juxtapositions of a vector or a matrix with a scalar `T`, in either order, a valid overloading beside the library's juxtapositions with a `String`. `Number` also declares the `asFloat` that the vector norm calls. The eight number types meet the bound. `Number` itself and the numeral type `IntLiteral` do not, since neither is a ring at its own type. So `Vector[\Number,n\]` is not a well-formed type, and `array1` and `array2` have no branch for `Number`: called at `Number`, they give a plain array at run time, not a vector or a matrix (F-229). The checker accepts a call whose argument's type is built from the caller's own type parameter under the same bound, such as the norm's `squaredNorm(me)` (F-227). The scalar extension, the eight operators between an array and a scalar, has bounds of its own, also with `Number` (F-119). Gated under walk by `tests/ArrayElementAlgebra.fss`. The bound is the default that `reviews/array-fork2-judgement.md` recommended. It landed when the curator said to land it, and his answer on the bound itself is pending (PLAN item 13, fork 2, among the array questions after the switch-over; review-queue item 31, which also gives the other way, a bound for each operation). Detail: that judgement, sections 2.5 (what `Number`'s `comprises` clause gives), 2.9, way 3 (the ring alone, measured) and 4 (the bound and its cost).
  by: the clarity pass; note: rewritten for a reader without the batch (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f0/proposals.md, "F-151"); one correction: Number's closed comprises clause makes an element type exclude String, which keeps the family's four scalar juxtapositions a valid overloading beside the library's String juxtapositions, where the old text tied it to the scalar block's mirror pairs, which keep Number in their own bounds (reviews/array-design-ways.md:271-277; FortressLibrary.fsi:2702-2712); added from the same sources: Number's asFloat, which the norm calls (array-design-ways.md:273; FortressLibrary.fss:2633), and which element types meet the bound (reviews/array-fork2-judgement.md section 2.9, way 4; FortressBuiltin.fsi:179), and that the bound landed at the curator's word to land it (review-queue item 31; PLAN item 11); the pending review pointed at PLAN item 13, fork 2, and review-queue item 31, in place of PLAN item 52, now in PLAN-history and about climb batch 13b's landing, and of P-85, whose three questions do not include the bound; the sentences on the first factories and the norm tied to the bound (F-229, row 686); moved here: gap-ledger rows 685 and 686, FIXED, the bound's two former costs at the checker
- F-263 **In the interpreter's library each integer power is declared to answer its base's own type; under walk, at a negative exponent, the bases 1 and -1 give their exact power, a base of 0 raises `DivisionByZero`, and any other base raises `IntegerOverflow`.** It builds P-153. Under walk the power answers its base's type and follows these rules at `ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `NN32`, `NN64` and `ZZ`, with an exponent of any integer type, including a `ZZ` exponent too large for 64 bits, such as 2^70. Under walk a numeral is a `ZZ32` value (F-74), so `2^(-1)` raises `IntegerOverflow`. `IntLiteral` is the type that the compiled checker gives an integer numeral (F-262). Under walk the name `IntLiteral` also denotes a value, a zero that no numeral reaches. Its power follows the same rules, but answers a `ZZ32`, as all its arithmetic does, against its declared `IntLiteral`: a gap of walk (gap-ledger row 578). `QQ`'s power takes an exponent of any integer type. For a fraction, write a float or rational base: `2.0^(-1)` is 0.5, and `(2/3)^(-1)` is `3/2`. The compiled checker accepts the library's own uses of these powers (the distance stage). microGPT's only integer power is in the library's `strToInt`, always at a nonnegative exponent. The compiled path's own library does not follow these rules. At a negative exponent its `ZZ32` power gives 0 at every base but 0, 1 and -1, and a raw Java `RuntimeException` at a base of 0 (row 441). P-153 lists this as still to build, with that power's overflow, which throws a raw Java exception that no Fortress `catch` sees, or saturates quietly (row 523). Tests: `tests/IntegerPowerContractWalk.fss`, `tests/QQPowerExponent.fss`; on the compiled path, the expected failure `compiler_tests/XXXIntegerPowerContractCompiled.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-integer-power/REPORT.md` sections 1, 2 and 4 (the change, the tests, the checker's sites); `perf-probes/integer-power/REPORT.md` section 1.6 (microGPT's powers).
  by: the clarity pass; note: rewritten for a reader without the batch (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f0/proposals.md, "F-263"); the title scoped to the interpreter's library and walk, as the body limits it; the exponent beyond 64 bits restored from the text of 2026-10-20 12:51Z and IntegerPowerContractWalk's exponentTypes, as a ZZ exponent such as 2^70; added: a numeral's 2^(-1) raises IntegerOverflow under walk (F-74; the test's ZZ32 case; the integer-power probe's contract table, row E); the IntLiteral value said from row 578; the compiled negative power narrowed to ZZ32's, 0 at every base but 0, 1 and -1 (row 441; CompilerBuiltin.fsi:267, :326, :385); row 523 named as the compiled overflow, which P-153 assigns it, and its failure said; dropped: 'contract E', the integer-power probe's letter for P-153's rule, and the claim that walk's values moved only at a negative exponent, broader than rung-integer-power/REPORT.md section 9, point 3; moved here: the distance's six power sites gone (31 to 25, the count 28 to 22, on rung I's tree), strToInt's fail[\ZZ32\] (row 747), the second export error 44 entries to 43 (MultiplicativeRing's api power), and the team tests realArith, compoundArray and simpleExp and the demos BiCGSTAB, BiCGSTAB2 and npbft rewritten to a float base (that report, sections 1, 4 and 9)
- F-264 **In the interpreter's library, nine `Vector` and `Matrix` operators and three branches of the array factories get their declared type through the function `cast`, and so does the sub-array that `Array2`'s column and row subscripts pass to `Col` and `Row`.** `cast[\T\](x)` returns `x` if it has type `T`, and raises `CastError` if not (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:33-37`). The operators are `Vector`'s `+`, binary and unary `-`, `scale` and `pmul`, and `Matrix`'s `+`, binary and unary `-` and `scale`. Each builds its result with the inherited `map` or `ivmap`, typed `Array1` or `Array2`, and so through `replica`, the method that makes an array of the same layout. The factory branches are those of `__builtinFactory1`, `__immutableFactory1` and `__builtinFactory2` for an array whose lowest indices are 0, each cast to its factory's declared result type. The subscripts are `a[:, j]` and `a[i, :]`. Each builds its sub-array with a range subscript, which answers an unsized `Array`, and the cast gives it the sized `Array2` that the field of `Col` or `Row` takes. Five bodies build with `fill` or `tabulate` and need no cast, since `Vector` and `Matrix` redeclare those two at their own type (F-158): the factories `vector(v)`, `tabulatedVector` and `matrix(v)`, and `Matrix`'s `rmul` and `lmul`. So the compiled checker accepts all these bodies (the distance stage). Under walk each cast holds at the eight number types (`tests/VectorMatrixCastsWalk.fss`). A `Vector` whose `replica` answers an array that is not a `Vector`, or a `Matrix` whose `replica` answers one that is not a `Matrix`, raises `CastError` at the nine operators. When the casts landed, no walk value moved in the 42 test files that use the array family, compared value by value, or in four array demos; every test of the interpreter's suite passed, and so did microGPT's quick pair. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-array-sites/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 5 (each change with its sites; walk's runs); `compile-ladder/rung-array-sites/SKEPTIC.md` sections 2 (the four demos) and 3, finding 3 (the `CastError` case).
